 "word_count": 146,
 "lines": [
  "Rome's favourite prince said he was murdered. So why does the Senate's own bronze verdict never call it murder?",
  "His name was Germanicus, the adopted son of the emperor Tiberius.",
  "He was sent east with authority over its governors, and Piso, the governor of Syria, defied him at every turn.",
  "Then Germanicus fell ill.",
  "The historian Tacitus says things were found hidden in the floor and walls: human remains, spells, and his name, written on sheets of lead.",
  "These were curse tablets, meant to hand a victim to the gods of the underworld.",
  "Dying, Germanicus blamed Piso.",
  "Piso was tried before the Senate, but before the verdict he was found dead, a sword beside him.",
  "The Senate judged him anyway, and ordered its decree cast in bronze and put up across the empire.",
  "It lists his crimes, one by one.",
  "Murder is not one of them."
 ],
